{"qas": [
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2604.09628, which XAI algorithms are top-ranked for both faithfulness and robustness under Art. 86 of the EU AI Act, and what procedural filter affects their admissibility?", "answer": "SHAP and RuleSHAP are top-ranked for faithfulness and robustness under Art. 86, and only methods with local, ex-post scope/stage are admissible due to the procedural filter.", "evidence": [{"page": 13, "quote": "For Art. 86 (local, ex-post justifications), SHAP, RuleSHAP, and CEM support highly faithful instance-level explanations; here, counterfactual examples, trees, and rule-based surrogates can be layered on top as presentation devices when audiences require low complexity, without using global methods as the primary compliance backbone."}, {"page": 11, "quote": "local ex-post methods are not admissible for provisions that require global ex-ante documentation, while global ex-ante methods are not admissible for provisions that require local ex-post justifications."}]},

  {"question": "In arXiv 2604.09628, which XAI methods are excluded from Art. 11 and Annex IV compliance due to their scope and stage, and which are the best low-complexity candidates?", "answer": "Anchors, CEM, and DiCE are excluded due to being local, ex-post; Decision Trees, RuleFit, and RuleSHAP are the best low-complexity candidates.", "evidence": [{"page": 13, "quote": "For Art. 11 and Annex IV, local ex-post methods such as Anchors, CEM, and DiCE are excluded by criterion D3, so the best admissible low-complexity candidates are Decision Trees, RuleFit, and RuleSHAP."}]},

  {"question": "According to arXiv 2604.09628, what are the three main clusters of XAI explanation properties, and what sub-properties are included under 'Faithfulness'?", "answer": "The three main clusters are Faithfulness, Robustness, and Complexity. Faithfulness includes Necessity/No False Positives, Sufficiency/No False Negatives, and Completeness/Coverage.", "evidence": [{"page": 6, "quote": "Faithfulness (also referred to as fidelity or correctness) captures how accurately an explanation reflects the model's actual reasoning. It includes: (i) Necessity/No False Positives, meaning that the explanation includes only those features or elements that genuinely influence the model's output; (ii) Sufficiency/No False Negatives, meaning that it excludes only features or elements that are irrelevant to the output; and (iii) Completeness/Coverage, meaning it covers the model's full rationale without omitting relevant factors."}]},

  {"question": "In arXiv 2604.09628, what does the abbreviation 'FP' stand for in the context of XAI algorithm evaluation tables?", "answer": "'FP' stands for 'False Positives', referring to features included in an explanation that do not actually influence the model's outcome.", "evidence": [{"page": 8, "quote": "| XAI Algorithm.XAI Algorithm   |   No FP |   Faithfulness.No FN |   Faithfulness.Completeness |"}, {"page": 6, "quote": "Necessity/No False Positives: Only features or elements that actually influence the model's outcome."}]},

  {"question": "Which XAI method, as described in arXiv 2604.09628, is noted for providing the most granular explanations but is highly sensitive to small data changes?", "answer": "Decision Trees offer very fine-grained, per-leaf explanations but are highly sensitive to small data changes.", "evidence": [{"page": 9, "quote": "Decision Trees  | Level of Detail (5): Very fine-grained, per-leaf explanations. | Stability (1): Highly sensitive to small data changes."}]},

  {"question": "According to arXiv 2604.09628, which XAI method is described as producing explanations that are both highly faithful and robust to adversarial changes, but whose outputs can vary significantly across runs?", "answer": "CEM produces highly faithful, robust explanations, but its outputs vary with solver seed, leading to low stability.", "evidence": [{"page": 9, "quote": "CEM | No FP (5): Produces highly faithful minimal changes. Completeness (4): Provides richer rationale than basic counterfactuals. Adv. Rob. (4): Explanations remain valid under perturbations. | Stability (1): Outputs vary with solver seed."}]}
]}
